WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.455
Marcus and Elena list what could go wrong financially.

00:00:03.455 --> 00:00:08.445
Marcus could be too hurt to work, which is a loss of income.

00:00:08.445 --> 00:00:12.667
Anyone could face hospital and physician bills, which is medical expense.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:16.506
The children need cleanings and fillings, which is dental expense.

00:00:16.506 --> 00:00:21.880
Elena’s mother may someday need help with daily living, which is long-term care expense.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.567
Glasses and prescriptions are smaller, predictable costs.

00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:26.870
Each need has a matching benefit.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:31.860
Health insurance is a family of products, not one product, and the exam

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.850
expects you to name the right member of the family for each loss.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.352
Vision benefits usually cover an exam and an

00:00:39.352 --> 00:00:41.853
allowance toward lenses or frames on a schedule.

00:00:41.853 --> 00:00:44.980
Prescription drug benefits may be built into a medical plan

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.107
or sold separately, with copayments that differ by drug tier.

00:00:48.107 --> 00:00:51.234
Specified disease, or dread disease, policies pay only if a

00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:54.361
named illness such as cancer is diagnosed, often as a

00:00:54.361 --> 00:00:57.175
lump sum or scheduled amount regardless of other coverage.

00:00:57.175 --> 00:00:59.676
They are supplements, not substitutes for medical insurance.

00:00:59.676 --> 00:01:02.803
A producer who presents a cancer policy as full health

00:01:02.803 --> 00:01:05.930
coverage misleads the buyer and may violate Maryland’s advertising rules.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.233
AD and D pays a principal sum if an accident

00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:12.536
causes death, usually within a stated period after the injury.

00:01:12.536 --> 00:01:15.839
It pays a capital sum, often half the principal sum,

00:01:15.839 --> 00:01:19.142
for loss of a limb or sight in one eye,

00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:22.445
and typically the full principal sum for two such losses.

00:01:22.445 --> 00:01:24.757
It pays nothing for death from sickness.

00:01:24.757 --> 00:01:27.729
AD and D is sold alone, as a rider,

00:01:27.729 --> 00:01:30.702
or as part of group plans and travel coverage.

00:01:30.702 --> 00:01:35.326
Because the benefit is a fixed amount rather than reimbursement of bills, it is

00:01:35.326 --> 00:01:39.950
a valued benefit, and the accident definition in Lesson 1 decides whether it responds.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.750
Rosa buys her own medical plan.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:49.282
The insurer underwrites her, she owns the policy and she pays the whole premium.

00:01:49.282 --> 00:01:51.148
That is individual coverage.

00:01:51.148 --> 00:01:53.481
Marcus is covered at work.

00:01:53.481 --> 00:02:00.014
His employer holds the master policy, he receives a certificate of coverage, the group

00:02:00.014 --> 00:02:06.546
is underwritten as a whole and the employer often pays part of the premium.

00:02:06.546 --> 00:02:08.412
That is group coverage.

00:02:08.412 --> 00:02:14.945
Group plans spread risk across the whole workforce, cost less per person to administer

00:02:14.945 --> 00:02:21.477
and have special rules about eligibility, continuation and conversion that we will study later.

00:02:21.477 --> 00:02:25.210
Individual plans follow the person wherever she goes.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:28.430
Rosa’s and Marcus’s plans are private: a company

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.650
accepts premium and promises benefits under a contract.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.272
Dennis, who turns 65 next year, will join Medicare,

00:02:35.272 --> 00:02:38.894
a federal program funded by payroll taxes and premiums.

00:02:38.894 --> 00:02:44.126
Gloria, his mother, might someday qualify for Medicaid if her savings run out.

00:02:44.126 --> 00:02:48.151
Social Security disability and workers compensation are also government programs.

00:02:48.151 --> 00:02:52.578
Private coverage is voluntary and contractual; government coverage is created by

00:02:52.578 --> 00:02:56.603
statute and open to people who meet the statutory tests.

00:02:56.603 --> 00:03:01.030
Many families hold both, and later lessons explain which pays first.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:06.032
A comprehensive medical plan covers a wide range of hospital,

00:03:06.032 --> 00:03:11.035
surgical, physician and preventive services, subject to deductibles and coinsurance.

00:03:11.035 --> 00:03:16.537
A limited policy covers only a narrow peril or pays a

00:03:16.537 --> 00:03:22.040
small fixed amount: accident-only, hospital indemnity, specified disease, dental-only or vision-only.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:28.543
Limited policies are legitimate supplements, but Maryland requires them to disclose their limited

00:03:28.543 --> 00:03:34.546
nature so no one mistakes a $200-a-day hospital policy for health insurance.

00:03:34.546 --> 00:03:39.048
When you classify a policy, use all three axes:

00:03:39.048 --> 00:03:43.550
individual or group, private or government, limited or comprehensive.
